const EXPLAIN={
1:{"how": ["Pick two rows: (−4, 10) and (−2, 7).", "<b>Run</b>: −2 − (−4) = 2. <b>Rise</b>: 7 − 10 = −3. Slope = <b>−<span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>2</span></span></b>.", "Find <var>b</var> with (6, −5): −5 = −<span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>2</span></span>(6) + <var>b</var>, so −5 = −9 + <var>b</var> and <var>b</var> = <b>4</b>.", "<var>y</var> = −<span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>2</span></span><var>x</var> + 4. That is A."], "why": {"1": "The slope is right, but the y-intercept isn’t. From −5 = −9 + b, add 9 to both sides: b = 4, not −14.", "2": "This slope is upside down (run over rise). Slope is change in y on top: −3 over 2.", "3": "This slope is upside down. Slope is change in y over change in x: −3/2. Check (−4, 10): −2/3(−4) − 1 is not 10."}, "right": "Slope from two rows, then plug a point in to find b.", "desmos": "Add a table with the points, then use <b>Add Regression</b> (linear): m = −1.5 and b = 4.", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Linear ax+b</b>. The Stats tab shows the equation: a = −1.5, b = 4."},
2:{"how": ["Use two points on the line: (0, 9) and (4, 8).", "<b>Run</b>: 4 − 0 = 4 hours. <b>Rise</b>: 8 − 9 = −1 meter.", "Rate = −1 ÷ 4 = <b>−0.25</b> meter per hour.", "Negative means the water level <b>decreases</b>, at a rate of <b>0.25</b> m/h."], "miss": "The line goes down, so the water level decreases. The rate is the change in water level divided by the change in time: 1 ÷ 4 = 0.25. The 4 is the hours, and 1 is the meters.", "right": "Rise over run, and the sign tells up or down."},
3:{"how": ["The parabola crosses the x-axis at <b>−3</b> and <b>1</b>, so the factors are (<var>x</var> + 3)(<var>x</var> − 1).", "Multiply: <var>x</var><sup>2</sup> − <var>x</var> + 3<var>x</var> − 3 = <b><var>x</var><sup>2</sup> + 2<var>x</var> − 3</b>.", "Check the y-intercept: at <var>x</var> = 0 it is −3, and the graph crosses the y-axis at −3. ✓ That is A."], "why": {"1": "This factors to (x − 3)(x + 1), so it crosses the x-axis at 3 and −1. The graph crosses at −3 and 1.", "2": "This factors to (x + 3)(x + 1), so it crosses at −3 and −1. It also crosses the y-axis at +3, but the graph crosses at −3.", "3": "This factors to (x − 3)(x − 1), so it crosses at 1 and 3. The graph crosses at −3 and 1."}, "right": "Zeros give the factors. Multiply them out.", "desmos": "Type a choice, like <b>y = x^2 + 2x − 3</b>. Its x-intercepts are (−3, 0) and (1, 0), and its lowest point is (−1, −4), just like the graph.", "numworks": "<b>Grapher</b>: Add an element, type x² + 2x − 3, open the Graph tab, press the <b>Toolbox</b> key and choose <b>Minimum</b>: (−1, −4), the same low point as the graph."},
4:{"how": ["Rectangle area = length × width = 10(5 + 2<var>x</var>) = 50 + 20<var>x</var>.", "Triangle area = <span class=\"frac\"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span> × base × height = <span class=\"frac\"><span>1</span><span>2</span></span>(30)(4<var>x</var> − 10) = 15(4<var>x</var> − 10) = 60<var>x</var> − 150.", "Set them equal: 50 + 20<var>x</var> = 60<var>x</var> − 150. Subtract 20<var>x</var>: 50 = 40<var>x</var> − 150. Add 150: 200 = 40<var>x</var>.", "Slice by 40: <var>x</var> = <b>5</b>. That is D.", "Check: rectangle 10 × 15 = 150, triangle ½ × 30 × 10 = 150. ✓"], "why": {"0": "From 50 = 40x − 150, add 150 to both sides: 200 = 40x. Subtracting gives 100, and x = 2.5 doesn’t make the areas equal.", "1": "The triangle’s area is <b>½</b> × base × height. Doubling instead of halving gives about 3.", "2": "This sets the length equal to the height. The question says the <b>areas</b> are equal, so use the area formulas."}, "right": "Write each area, set them equal, then solve.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 10(5 + 2x)</b> and <b>y = (1/2)(30)(4x − 10)</b>. Click where they cross: (5, 150).", "numworks": "<b>Equations</b>: type the equation (with =), then <b>Solve the equation</b>: 10(5 + 2x) = (1/2)(30)(4x − 10) gives x = 5."},
5:{"how": ["Pull out the 2: 2(15<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> − 2<var>x</var> − 8).", "Multiply 15 × (−8) = −120. Two numbers that multiply to −120 and add to −2: <b>−12</b> and <b>10</b>.", "Split and group: 15<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> − 12<var>x</var> + 10<var>x</var> − 8 = 3<var>x</var>(5<var>x</var> − 4) + 2(5<var>x</var> − 4) = (5<var>x</var> − 4)(3<var>x</var> + 2).", "So 30<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> − 4<var>x</var> − 16 = 2(5<var>x</var> − 4)(3<var>x</var> + 2). The factor in the choices is <b>5<var>x</var> − 4</b>. That is C."], "why": {"0": "(5x + 4)(3x − 2) = 15x² + 2x − 8. The middle term is +2x, but we need −2x. The signs are swapped.", "1": "3x − 2 goes with 5x + 4, and (5x + 4)(3x − 2) has a middle term of +2x. We need −2x.", "3": "(5x + 2)(3x − 4) = 15x² − 14x − 8. The middle term should be −2x."}, "right": "Pull out what every term shares, then factor the rest.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 30x^2 − 4x − 16</b>. Click the graph to see its gray points: it crosses the x-axis at (0.8, 0), and 0.8 = 4/5 comes from 5x − 4 = 0.", "numworks": "<b>Equations</b>: type the equation (with =), then <b>Solve the equation</b>: 30x² − 4x − 16 = 0 gives x = −2/3 and x = 4/5. 5x − 4 = 0 gives 4/5."},
6:{"how": ["Find the correlation coefficient, <var>r</var>, with a linear regression on the table.", "For this data, <var>r</var> ≈ <b>−0.09</b>.", "Negative <var>r</var> means negative. But −0.09 is very close to 0, so the association is <b>weak</b>. That is B."], "why": {"0": "r for this data is about −0.09, not −0.91. And r ≈ −0.91 would be strong, not weak.", "2": "r for this data is about −0.09, not −0.91. The points jump up and down a lot.", "3": "r ≈ −0.09 is close to 0. Close to 0 means weak, not strong."}, "right": "Close to 1 or −1 = strong. Close to 0 = weak.", "desmos": "Add a table with the data, then use <b>Add Regression</b> (linear). It shows r ≈ −0.09.", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Linear ax+b</b>. The Stats tab shows r ≈ −0.09."},
7:{"how": ["<b>Range</b> is the <b>y</b>-values. Read the graph from <b>bottom to top</b>.", "The bottom end is an <b>open</b> circle at <var>y</var> = −4, so −4 is <b>not</b> included: <b>greater than −4</b>.", "The top end is a <b>closed</b> dot at <var>y</var> = 2, so 2 <b>is</b> included: <b>less than or equal to 2</b>."], "miss": "Open circle means the point is not included (no “or equal”). Closed dot means it is included. Range uses the y-values (−4 to 2), not the x-values (−2 to 3).", "right": "Range: bottom to top. Open circle = not included."},
8:{"how": ["4<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> = (2<var>x</var>)<sup>2</sup> and 81 = 9<sup>2</sup>. Check the middle: 2 × 2<var>x</var> × 9 = 36<var>x</var>. ✓", "So it is a perfect square: 4<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> − 36<var>x</var> + 81 = (2<var>x</var> − 9)<sup>2</sup>.", "Set 2<var>x</var> − 9 = 0: 2<var>x</var> = 9, so <var>x</var> = <b><span class=\"frac\"><span>9</span><span>2</span></span></b>. That is D."], "why": {"0": "Multiply it out: (4x − 3)(x − 27) = 4x² − 111x + 81. The middle term should be −36x.", "1": "Multiply it out: (2x + 3)(2x + 27) = 4x² + 60x + 81. The middle term should be −36x.", "2": "(2x − 9)(2x + 9) = 4x² − 81. That is a difference of squares with no middle term."}, "right": "Check for a perfect square: (first)² − 2(first)(last) + (last)².", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 4x^2 − 36x + 81</b>. It touches the x-axis at one point, (4.5, 0), and 4.5 = 9/2.", "numworks": "<b>Equations</b>: type the equation (with =), then <b>Solve the equation</b>: 4x² − 36x + 81 = 0 gives x = 9/2."},
9:{"how": ["In <var>y</var> = 16(0.75)<sup><var>x</var></sup>, each step multiplies by 0.75, so the values keep getting smaller.", "They get closer and closer to 0 but never reach it.", "The graph flattens out along the horizontal line <b><var>y</var> = 0</b>."], "miss": "An exponential like y = a(b)ˣ with nothing added at the end has the asymptote y = 0. 16 is the y-intercept and 0.75 is what you multiply by. The asymptote is horizontal, so it starts with y =.", "right": "Nothing added at the end: asymptote y = 0.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 16(0.75)^x</b> and zoom out. The right side flattens along the x-axis, y = 0.", "numworks": "<b>Grapher</b>: Add an element, type 16(0.75)^x, open the Graph tab. The curve flattens toward y = 0."},
10:{"how": ["Power to a power: <b>multiply</b> the exponents.", "<span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>7</span></span> × 2 = <b><span class=\"frac\"><span>6</span><span>7</span></span></b>.", "So it is <var>x</var><sup><span class=\"frac\"><span>6</span><span>7</span></span></sup>. That is C."], "why": {"0": "6/14 is the same as 3/7. You doubled the top and the bottom, which doesn’t change anything. Multiply only the top: 3 × 2 = 6.", "1": "You added: 3/7 + 2 = 17/7. Power to a power means multiply.", "3": "You added 2 to the top: 3 + 2 = 5. Power to a power means multiply: 3 × 2 = 6."}, "right": "Power to a power: multiply the exponents.", "numworks": "<b>Calculation</b>: try x = 2. Type (2^(3/7))^(2) and 2^(6/7) (after each exponent, press the right arrow key to come back down). Both show about 1.811."},
11:{"how": ["Vertex: <var>x</var> = −<span class=\"frac\"><span><var>b</var></span><span>2<var>a</var></span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>20</span><span>10</span></span> = <b>2</b>. Then <var>n</var>(2) = 5(4) − 40 + 12 = <b>−8</b>.", "The number in front of <var>x</var><sup>2</sup> is positive, so it opens <b>up</b>. The lowest point is (2, −8).", "Range: every <var>y</var>-value from −8 up: <var>n</var>(<var>x</var>) ≥ −8. That is B.", "(The domain of every quadratic function is all real numbers.)"], "why": {"0": "2 is the x-value of the vertex. The domain of a quadratic is all real numbers: the graph keeps going left and right.", "2": "The graph keeps going to the left too, so the domain is all real numbers, not just x ≥ 0.", "3": "12 is the y-intercept. The parabola opens up, so the range goes up from its minimum, −8."}, "right": "Find the vertex, then check which way it opens.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 5x^2 − 20x + 12</b>. Click the graph to see its gray points: the lowest point is (2, −8).", "numworks": "<b>Grapher</b>: Add an element, type 5x² − 20x + 12, open the Graph tab, press the <b>Toolbox</b> key and choose <b>Minimum</b>: (2, −8)."},
12:{"how": ["Hats: $7 each, <var>x</var> hats: 7<var>x</var>. T-shirts: $10 each, <var>y</var> T-shirts: 10<var>y</var>.", "Total: 7<var>x</var> + 10<var>y</var>.", "“Over $100” means more than 100: <b>&gt; 100</b>.", "7<var>x</var> + 10<var>y</var> &gt; 100."], "miss": "Each price goes with its own item: 7 with hats (x) and 10 with T-shirts (y). 17 is the two prices added together. “Over” means more than, so the sign is &gt;, not = or &lt;.", "right": "Price times amount for each, and “over” means &gt;.", "check": "Try 10 hats and 4 T-shirts: 7(10) + 10(4) = $110, which is over $100. ✓"},
13:{"how": ["The question says a <b>quadratic</b> function fits the data, so use quadratic regression.", "The model is about <var>y</var> = −0.00734<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> + 0.689<var>x</var> + 14.115.", "Put in <var>x</var> = 65: about <b>27.9</b> miles per gallon. That is A.", "It makes sense: the miles per gallon go up and then start coming back down."], "why": {"1": "31.0 comes from a <b>linear</b> regression. The question says to use a quadratic function.", "2": "40.2 comes from a line through only the first two rows. Use all the data with quadratic regression.", "3": "24.9 is the miles per gallon at 20 miles per hour, the first row. The question asks about 65."}, "right": "Quadratic data: use quadratic regression, then plug in.", "desmos": "Add a table with the data, then use <b>Add Regression</b> and pick quadratic. To predict, type <b>a·65^2 + b·65 + c</b>: about 27.9.", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Quadratic ax²+bx+c</b>. Then press the <b>Toolbox</b> key, choose <b>Predict Y given X</b>, and type 65: about 27.9."},
14:{"how": ["Exponential: <var>p</var>(<var>x</var>) = <var>a</var>(<var>b</var>)<sup><var>x</var></sup>. <var>a</var> is the starting value: <b>48,000</b>.", "<var>b</var> is what you multiply by each year: next ÷ previous = <b><span class=\"frac\"><span>50,400</span><span>48,000</span></span></b> (which is 1.05).", "<var>p</var>(<var>x</var>) = 48,000(<span class=\"frac\"><span>50,400</span><span>48,000</span></span>)<sup><var>x</var></sup>. That is A."], "why": {"1": "50,400 is the population after 1 year. The starting value a is the population at x = 0: 48,000.", "2": "The start is 48,000, not 50,400. And b is next ÷ previous, 50,400/48,000. A b of 1/48,000 would shrink the town almost to 0.", "3": "The start is right, but b is next ÷ previous: 50,400/48,000. A b of 1/50,400 would shrink the town almost to 0."}, "right": "a = start (x = 0). b = next ÷ previous.", "numworks": "<b>Calculation</b>: type 48000×(50400/48000). It shows 50400, the population after 1 year."},
15:{"how": ["<var>g</var>(<var>x</var>) = 3<var>f</var>(<var>x</var>) = 3<var>x</var>. Multiplying by 3 makes every <var>y</var>-value 3 times as big.", "So <var>g</var> is <b>steeper</b> than <var>f</var> and still goes through (0, 0).", "At <var>x</var> = 1, <var>f</var> is at 1 and <var>g</var> is at 3. That is D."], "why": {"0": "This g is f(x) − 3: the same steepness, moved down 3.", "1": "This g is f(x) + 3: the same steepness, moved up 3.", "2": "This g is (1/3)x, which is less steep. 3f(x) multiplies every y by 3, so g is steeper."}, "right": "A number multiplied in front changes the steepness.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = x</b> and <b>y = 3x</b>. Both go through (0, 0), and y = 3x is steeper.", "numworks": "<b>Grapher</b>: Add an element, type x, then add 3x, and open the Graph tab. Both go through (0, 0), and 3x is steeper."},
16:{"how": ["Pull out the 4 from the top: 4(2<var>w</var><sup>2</sup> − 5<var>w</var> − 3).", "Factor: 2<var>w</var><sup>2</sup> − 5<var>w</var> − 3 = (2<var>w</var> + 1)(<var>w</var> − 3).", "Now <span class=\"frac\"><span>4(2<var>w</var> + 1)(<var>w</var> − 3)</span><span>2<var>w</var> + 1</span></span>: the (2<var>w</var> + 1) cancels.", "Left: 4(<var>w</var> − 3) = <b>4<var>w</var> − 12</b>. That is B."], "why": {"0": "Check: (4w + 12)(2w + 1) = 8w² + 28w + 12. That isn’t the top.", "2": "You dropped the 4 you pulled out. (w − 3)(2w + 1) is only one-fourth of the top.", "3": "The 4 was dropped, and the sign is off: 2w² − 5w − 3 = (2w + 1)(w − 3), not (w + 3)."}, "right": "Factor the top, cancel what matches the bottom.", "check": "Try w = 1: (8 − 20 − 12) ÷ (2 + 1) = −24 ÷ 3 = −8, and 4(1) − 12 = −8. ✓", "numworks": "<b>Calculation</b>: try w = 1. Type (8×1^(2) − 20×1 − 12)/(2×1 + 1) and 4×1 − 12 (after each exponent, press the right arrow key to come back down). Both show −8."},
17:{"how": ["Test each point in <var>y</var> ≤ <span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>5</span></span><var>x</var> − 6. If the statement is true, the point is in the solution set.", "(5, −4): <span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>5</span></span>(5) − 6 = 3 − 6 = −3. Is −4 ≤ −3? <b>Yes.</b> That is A."], "why": {"1": "(−2, −5): 3/5(−2) − 6 = −7.2. Is −5 ≤ −7.2? No, −5 is bigger.", "2": "(9, 1): 3/5(9) − 6 = −0.6. Is 1 ≤ −0.6? No.", "3": "(−8, 3): 3/5(−8) − 6 = −10.8. Is 3 ≤ −10.8? No."}, "right": "Plug the point in. True means it is in the solution set.", "desmos": "The test version won’t graph inequalities. Type the boundary line <b>y = (3/5)x − 6</b> and the point (5, −4): it is below the line, and ≤ means on or below."},
18:{"how": ["You can put any <var>x</var> into an exponential function, so the <b>domain is all real numbers</b>.", "4<sup><var>x</var></sup> is always positive. −7 times a positive number is always <b>negative</b>.", "The values get close to 0 but never reach it. So the <b>range is all real numbers less than 0</b>. That is B."], "why": {"0": "4 is the base b, not a limit on x. Any x works, so the domain is all real numbers.", "2": "Any x works, so the domain is all real numbers. And the y-values never reach 0.", "3": "−7 times a positive number is always negative, so the range is only numbers less than 0."}, "right": "Domain: all real numbers. Range: one side of the asymptote.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = −7(4)^x</b> and zoom out. The whole graph is below the x-axis and flattens toward y = 0 on the left.", "numworks": "<b>Grapher</b>: Add an element, type −7(4)^x, open the Graph tab. The whole curve is below the x-axis."},
19:{"how": ["The highest point (vertex) is <b>(1, 4)</b>.", "The <b>axis of symmetry</b> is the vertical line through the vertex: <b><var>x</var> = 1</b> (VUX). That is D.", "Check the others: it crosses the x-axis only twice (−1 and 3), and the maximum value is 4."], "why": {"0": "The graph crosses the x-axis only twice, at −1 and 3. A parabola never has 3 zeros.", "1": "The highest point is (1, 4), so the maximum value is 4. 3 is where the graph crosses an axis, not the top.", "2": "The coordinates are switched. The vertex is (1, 4)."}, "right": "Axis of symmetry: x = the x-value of the vertex."},
20:{"how": ["Find the biggest perfect square that goes into 600: 100 × 6 = 600.", "√600 = √100 · √6 = <b>10√6</b>. That is B."], "why": {"0": "The numbers are switched. The 100 comes out as 10, and the 6 stays inside: 10√6.", "2": "24√25 = 24 × 5 = 120. But √600 is only about 24.5.", "3": "25√24 is about 122. Only the square root of a perfect square comes out: √25 = 5, not 25."}, "right": "Pull out the biggest perfect square.", "numworks": "<b>Calculation</b>: type √(600). It shows 10√6 (and 24.49 as a decimal)."},
21:{"how": ["The line goes straight up and down: it is <b>vertical</b>.", "<b>VUX</b>: a <b>V</b>ertical line has <b>U</b>ndefined slope and its equation is <b><var>x</var></b> = a number.", "It crosses the x-axis at −4, so the equation is <var>x</var> = −4, and the slope is undefined. That is A."], "why": {"1": "y = −4 is a horizontal line (HOY). And a vertical line’s slope is undefined, not −4.", "2": "The equation is right, but a vertical line’s slope is undefined. −4 is where it crosses the x-axis.", "3": "The slope is right, but y = −4 is a horizontal line. A vertical line is x = a number."}, "right": "VUX: vertical, undefined slope, x = ."},
22:{"how": ["The <b>zero</b> is where the line crosses the <b>x-axis</b> (where <var>y</var> = 0).", "Follow the line to the x-axis. It crosses at <b>2</b>. That is C."], "why": {"0": "−3 is the opposite of the slope. The zero is where the line crosses the x-axis.", "1": "3 is the slope (the line goes up 3 for every 1 to the right).", "3": "−6 is where the line crosses the <b>y</b>-axis. The zero is on the x-axis."}, "right": "Zero = x-intercept: where it crosses the x-axis."},
23:{"how": ["Multiply the first equation by 2: −6<var>x</var> + 10<var>y</var> = 42.", "Add it to the second: (−6<var>x</var> + 6<var>x</var>) + (10<var>y</var> − <var>y</var>) = 42 + (−15), so 9<var>y</var> = 27 and <var>y</var> = <b>3</b>.", "Put <var>y</var> = 3 in the second: 6<var>x</var> − 3 = −15, so 6<var>x</var> = −12 and <var>x</var> = <b>−2</b>.", "Solution (−2, 3). That is B.", "Check: −3(−2) + 5(3) = 6 + 15 = 21 ✓ and 6(−2) − 3 = −15 ✓."], "why": {"0": "Check it: −3(2) + 5(27) = 129, not 21.", "2": "Check it: −3(2) + 5(0) = −6, not 21. (It fits 3x + 4y = 6, which you get by adding the equations without canceling a variable.)", "3": "Check it: −3(−3) + 5(−3) = −6, not 21."}, "right": "Cancel one variable, solve, then check in both equations.", "desmos": "Solve both for y first: type <b>y = (21 + 3x)/5</b> and <b>y = 6x + 15</b>. Click where they cross: (−2, 3).", "numworks": "<b>Grapher</b>: add both lines in y = form (y = (21 + 3x)/5 and y = 6x + 15), open the Graph tab, press the <b>Toolbox</b> key and choose <b>Intersection</b>: (−2, 3)."},
24:{"how": ["Pull out the 12: 12(<var>x</var><sup>2</sup> − 4<var>x</var> + 4). That is <b>E</b>.", "Factor the inside: two numbers that multiply to 4 and add to −4: −2 and −2. So 12(<var>x</var> − 2)(<var>x</var> − 2) = 12(<var>x</var> − 2)<sup>2</sup>. That is <b>A</b>."], "why": {"1": "Multiply it out: −12(x² + 4x + 4) = −12x² − 48x − 48. Every sign is flipped.", "2": "Multiply it out: 12(x − 4)(x − 1) = 12x² − 60x + 48. The middle term should be −48x.", "3": "Multiply it out: −12(x + 2)² = −12x² − 48x − 48. Every sign is flipped."}, "right": "Pull out the 12, factor what is left, then multiply to check.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 12x^2 − 48x + 48</b>, then a choice. If the two graphs land exactly on top of each other, they are equivalent.", "numworks": "<b>Equations</b>: type the equation (with =), then <b>Solve the equation</b>: 12x² − 48x + 48 = 0 gives x = 2, which matches the factor (x − 2)."},
25:{"how": ["Vertex form: <var>y</var> = <var>a</var>(<var>x</var> − <var>h</var>)<sup>2</sup> + <var>k</var>. The vertex (1, 46) gives <var>y</var> = <var>a</var>(<var>x</var> − 1)<sup>2</sup> + 46.", "Plug in (3, 10): 10 = <var>a</var>(3 − 1)<sup>2</sup> + 46 = 4<var>a</var> + 46.", "Subtract 46: −36 = 4<var>a</var>, so <var>a</var> = <b>−9</b>.", "<var>y</var> = −9(<var>x</var> − 1)<sup>2</sup> + 46. That is D."], "why": {"0": "(3, 10) is just a point on the graph, not the vertex. And a is negative: the point is below the vertex, so it opens down.", "1": "Check (3, 10): 9(3 − 1)² + 46 = 82, not 10. a must be −9.", "2": "a is right, but this uses (3, 10) as the vertex. The vertex is (1, 46)."}, "right": "Vertex goes in, then use the point to find a.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = −9(x − 1)^2 + 46</b> and the point <b>(3, 10)</b>. The point lands on the curve, and the top is (1, 46).", "numworks": "<b>Grapher</b>: Add an element, type −9(x − 1)² + 46, open the Graph tab, press the <b>Toolbox</b> key and choose <b>Maximum</b>: (1, 46)."},
26:{"how": ["The data goes down by about the same amount each hour, so use <b>linear regression</b>.", "The line of best fit is about <var>y</var> = −41.1<var>x</var> + 337.3.", "Rounded: <var>f</var>(<var>x</var>) = 337 − 41<var>x</var>. That is B."], "why": {"0": "The slope is right, but 296 is the number at hour 1. The y-intercept is the value at x = 0, about 337.", "2": "This uses only the last two rows. Use all the data with linear regression.", "3": "This mixes the last two rows (slope −38) with the first value (296). Use all the data."}, "right": "Use all the data: linear regression.", "desmos": "Add a table with the data, then use <b>Add Regression</b> (linear): m ≈ −41.1 and b ≈ 337.3.", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Linear ax+b</b>. The Stats tab shows the equation: a ≈ −41.1, b ≈ 337.3."},
27:{"how": ["In <var>w</var>(<var>t</var>) = <var>a</var>(<var>b</var>)<sup><var>t</var></sup>, <var>a</var> = 270 is the starting number of whales.", "<var>b</var> = 1.029 is what you multiply by each year.", "1.029 is <b>greater than 1</b>, so the number grows. 1.029 is the <b>growth factor</b> (2.9% more each year). That is D."], "why": {"0": "The initial number is 270, the number in front.", "1": "1.029 is greater than 1, so the number of whales grows. A decay factor is less than 1.", "2": "At the end of the first year there are 270(1.029) ≈ 278 whales, not 1.029."}, "right": "a = start. b > 1 = growth factor.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 270(1.029)^x</b>. It crosses the y-axis at 270 and goes up."},
28:{"how": ["<b>Run</b> (always to the right, so positive): 4 − (−2) = <b>6</b>.", "<b>Rise</b>: 2 − (−2) = <b>4</b>. The line goes up.", "Slope = rise over run = <span class=\"frac\"><span>4</span><span>6</span></span> = <b><span class=\"frac\"><span>2</span><span>3</span></span></b>. That is C."], "why": {"0": "The size is right, but the line goes up from (−2, −2) to (4, 2), so the slope is positive.", "1": "This is upside down. Slope is rise over run: 4 over 6.", "3": "This is upside down and the wrong sign. Rise over run: 4/6 = 2/3."}, "right": "Run first (positive), then rise.", "desmos": "Add a table with (−2, −2) and (4, 2), then use <b>Add Regression</b> (linear): m ≈ 0.667, which is 2/3.", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Linear ax+b</b>. The Stats tab shows the equation: a ≈ 0.667, which is 2/3."},
29:{"how": ["Distribute both sides: 10<var>w</var> + 20 = 8<var>w</var> + 36.", "<b>Slide</b> the 8<var>w</var>: 2<var>w</var> + 20 = 36. Subtract 20: 2<var>w</var> = 16.", "<b>Slice</b> by 2: <var>w</var> = <b>8</b>. That is B.", "Check: 5(16 + 4) = 100 and 4(16 + 9) = 100. ✓"], "why": {"0": "You only multiplied the first term in each parentheses. The 5 multiplies the 4 too (20), and the 4 multiplies the 9 too (36).", "2": "When you move 8w to the other side, subtract it. Adding gives 18w and the wrong answer.", "3": "You added the outside numbers instead of multiplying, and only to the first terms. Distribute: 5 × 2w and 5 × 4."}, "right": "Distribute everything, slide, slice, then check.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 5(2x + 4)</b> and <b>y = 4(2x + 9)</b>. Click where they cross: (8, 100), so w = 8.", "numworks": "<b>Equations</b>: type the equation (with =), then <b>Solve the equation</b>: 5(2x + 4) = 4(2x + 9) gives x = 8."},
30:{"how": ["The graphed line is the first equation: 153<var>x</var> + 47<var>y</var> = 2,649.34, where <var>x</var> is the standard price and <var>y</var> is the discounted price.", "The second equation is <var>y</var> = 0.80<var>x</var>. Graph it on the same grid.", "They cross at about (13.9, 11.1).", "The question asks for the discounted price, which is <var>y</var>: about $11.1. The closest choice is <b>$11.15</b>. That is D."], "why": {"0": "$13.95 is about the standard ticket price (x). The question asks for the discounted price (y).", "1": "This is close to the standard ticket price (x), and a bit too high. The question asks for the discounted price (y).", "2": "This is 2,649.34 ÷ 200, the average price of all tickets. Find where the two lines cross."}, "right": "Find where the lines cross, then answer with the variable they ask for.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = (2649.34 − 153x)/47</b> and <b>y = 0.8x</b>. Click where they cross: about (13.9, 11.1).", "numworks": "<b>Grapher</b>: add both lines in y = form (y = (2649.34 − 153x)/47 and y = 0.8x), open the Graph tab, press the <b>Toolbox</b> key and choose <b>Intersection</b>: about (13.9, 11.1)."},
31:{"how": ["The first term is <var>f</var>(1) = <b>27</b>.", "Each next term is <span class=\"frac\"><span>1</span><span>3</span></span> of the one before (divide by 3).", "27 ÷ 3 = 9, 9 ÷ 3 = 3, 3 ÷ 3 = 1. So 27, 9, 3, 1. That is A."], "why": {"1": "This multiplies by 3. The rule multiplies by 1/3, which divides by 3.", "2": "The first term is f(1) = 27, not 1/3. Then multiply by 1/3 each time.", "3": "The first term is f(1) = 27, not 1/3. Then multiply by 1/3 each time."}, "right": "Start at f(1), then follow the rule one step at a time.", "numworks": "<b>Calculation</b>: type 27/3, then 9/3, then 3/3. You get 9, 3, 1."},
32:{"how": ["<var>g</var>(1) = 4 and <var>g</var>(2) = 4, so the vertex is halfway between: <var>x</var> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>2</span></span>. The table gives <var>g</var>(<span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>2</span></span>) = <b><span class=\"frac\"><span>19</span><span>4</span></span></b>.", "The values go up to <span class=\"frac\"><span>19</span><span>4</span></span> and then come back down, so the parabola opens <b>down</b>. <span class=\"frac\"><span>19</span><span>4</span></span> is the maximum.", "Range: every <var>y</var>-value up to <span class=\"frac\"><span>19</span><span>4</span></span>: all real numbers less than or equal to <span class=\"frac\"><span>19</span><span>4</span></span>. That is B."], "why": {"0": "3/2 is the x-value of the vertex. Range uses the y-value: 19/4.", "2": "3/2 is the x-value, and the parabola opens down, so the range is “less than or equal to.”", "3": "19/4 is right, but the parabola opens down (19/4 is the top), so the range is “less than or equal to.”"}, "right": "Find the top or bottom y-value, then check which way it opens.", "desmos": "Add a table with the points, then use <b>Add Regression</b> and pick quadratic. The highest point is (1.5, 4.75), and 4.75 = 19/4.", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Quadratic ax²+bx+c</b>. The curve opens down with its top at y = 4.75 (19/4)."},
33:{"how": ["Multiply each term: 2<var>a</var> × 3<var>a</var> = 6<var>a</var><sup>2</sup>, 2<var>a</var> × (−2) = −4<var>a</var>, 5 × 3<var>a</var> = 15<var>a</var>, 5 × (−2) = −10.", "Combine: 6<var>a</var><sup>2</sup> − 4<var>a</var> + 15<var>a</var> − 10 = <b>6<var>a</var><sup>2</sup> + 11<var>a</var> − 10</b>. That is C."], "why": {"0": "2a × 3a is 6a², not 6a.", "1": "This only multiplies the first terms and the last terms. There are four products, not two.", "3": "Keep the minus sign: 2a × (−2) = −4a and 5 × (−2) = −10."}, "right": "Four multiplications, then combine like terms.", "numworks": "<b>Calculation</b>: try a = 1. Type (2×1 + 5)(3×1 − 2) and 6×1^(2) + 11×1 − 10 (after the exponent, press the right arrow key to come back down). Both show 7."},
34:{"how": ["Each cartridge costs $18.99, and there are <var>c</var> cartridges: 18.99<var>c</var>.", "The shipping fee is $7.95 once per order, no matter how many: + 7.95.", "The total cost is <var>t</var>: <var>t</var> = 18.99<var>c</var> + 7.95. That is D."], "why": {"0": "The variables are switched (t is the total cost), and the fee is added to every cartridge. Shipping is charged once.", "1": "The variables are switched. t is the total cost, so the equation starts t =.", "2": "This adds the $7.95 fee to every cartridge. Shipping is charged once per order."}, "right": "Rate times amount, plus the one-time fee.", "check": "2 cartridges: 18.99(2) + 7.95 = 37.98 + 7.95 = $45.93. ✓"},
35:{"how": ["Dividing by a fraction is multiplying by its flip: <var>x</var><sup>6</sup><var>y</var><sup>3</sup> × <span class=\"frac\"><span><var>y</var></span><span><var>x</var><sup>2</sup></span></span>.", "<var>x</var>: dividing powers means subtract: 6 − 2 = <b>4</b>.", "<var>y</var>: the <var>y</var> moves up to the top, so add: 3 + 1 = <b>4</b>.", "<var>x</var><sup>4</sup><var>y</var><sup>4</sup>."], "miss": "The y is on the bottom of the fraction you divide by, so it ends up multiplying: 3 + 1 = 4, not 3 − 1 = 2. For x: 6 − 2 = 4, not 8 (that would be multiplying instead of dividing).", "right": "Divide: subtract exponents. Divide by a fraction: flip it.", "numworks": "<b>Calculation</b>: try x = 2, y = 3. Type 2^(6)×3^(3)÷(2^(2)/3) and 2^(4)×3^(4) (after each exponent, press the right arrow key to come back down). Both show 1296."},
36:{"how": ["It starts at <b>317</b> students, so <var>a</var> = 317.", "Increasing by 4% each year means keep 100% and add 4%: 104% = <b>1.04</b>.", "Growth multiplies every year, so <var>x</var> goes in the exponent: <var>f</var>(<var>x</var>) = 317(1.04)<sup><var>x</var></sup>. That is C."], "why": {"0": "4% as a decimal is 0.04, and growth is 1 + 0.04 = 1.04. A b of 4 would quadruple the number every year.", "1": "This adds 4 students each year. A percent increase multiplies, so it is exponential.", "3": "1.04 is right, but it multiplies x here. Each year is another × 1.04, so x is the exponent."}, "right": "Growth: 1 + the rate as a decimal, with x as the exponent.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 317(1.04)^x</b>. At x = 1 it is 329.68, which is 4% more than 317."},
37:{"how": ["Line <var>a</var>: (−11, 81) to (−6, 51): run 5, rise −30, slope <b>−6</b>. Using (4, −9): −9 = −6(4) + <var>b</var>, so <var>b</var> = 15. <var>y</var> = −6<var>x</var> + 15 → <b>6<var>x</var> + <var>y</var> = 15</b>.", "Line <var>b</var>: (−9, 18) to (−4, 3): run 5, rise −15, slope <b>−3</b>. Using (1, −12): −12 = −3 + <var>b</var>, so <var>b</var> = −9. <var>y</var> = −3<var>x</var> − 9 → <b>3<var>x</var> + <var>y</var> = −9</b>.", "That is D."], "why": {"0": "x − 6y = −90 is y = (1/6)x + 15, which goes up. Line a goes down with slope −6.", "1": "x + 6y = 90 is y = −(1/6)x + 15. The slope should be −6, not −1/6.", "2": "6x − y = −15 is y = 6x + 15, which goes up. Both lines go down (negative slopes)."}, "right": "Find each slope and y-intercept, then rearrange.", "desmos": "Add a table with Line a’s points, then use <b>Add Regression</b> (linear): m = −6, b = 15. Do the same for Line b: m = −3, b = −9.", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Linear ax+b</b>. The Stats tab shows the equation for each table: Line a gives a = −6, b = 15; Line b gives a = −3, b = −9."},
38:{"how": ["The parent function <var>f</var>(<var>x</var>) = <var>x</var><sup>2</sup> has its vertex at (0, 0).", "Inside, <var>x</var> − 3 moves the graph <b>right 3</b>. Inside does the opposite of the sign.", "Outside, − 9 moves the graph <b>down 9</b>.", "The vertex is <b>(3, −9)</b>."], "miss": "The x-value is the tricky one: x − 3 inside moves the graph right, so the x-value is +3, not −3. The − 9 is outside, so it moves the graph down 9.", "right": "Inside: opposite and sideways. Outside: up or down as it looks.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = (x − 3)^2 − 9</b>. Click the graph to see its gray points: the lowest point is (3, −9).", "numworks": "<b>Grapher</b>: Add an element, type (x − 3)² − 9, open the Graph tab, press the <b>Toolbox</b> key and choose <b>Minimum</b>: (3, −9)."},
39:{"how": ["Every term has a 6 and a <var>g</var> in it, so the greatest common factor is <b>6<var>g</var></b>.", "Divide each term by 6<var>g</var>: 24<var>g</var><var>h</var> ÷ 6<var>g</var> = 4<var>h</var>, 12<var>g</var><sup>2</sup> ÷ 6<var>g</var> = 2<var>g</var>, 18<var>g</var> ÷ 6<var>g</var> = 3.", "6<var>g</var>(4<var>h</var> − 2<var>g</var> + 3). That is C."], "why": {"0": "The 6g has to come out of every term. 12g² ÷ 6g = 2g and 18g ÷ 6g = 3.", "1": "12g doesn’t go into 18g evenly, and the last two terms weren’t divided at all.", "3": "Multiply it back: 12g × 3 = 36g, not 18g. 12g is too big to pull out of 18g."}, "right": "Find what every term shares, then divide each term by it.", "check": "Try g = 1, h = 1: 24 − 12 + 18 = 30, and 6(4 − 2 + 3) = 30. ✓"},
40:{"how": ["Let <var>x</var> = cost per click on Website 1 and <var>y</var> = cost per click on Website 2.", "Monday: 15<var>x</var> + 29<var>y</var> = 94.15. Tuesday: 25<var>x</var> + 29<var>y</var> = 121.15.", "Subtract Monday from Tuesday: the 29<var>y</var> cancels. 10<var>x</var> = 27, so <var>x</var> = <b>2.70</b>.", "Website 1 costs $2.70 per click. That is D."], "why": {"0": "$1.85 is the cost per click on Website 2 (y). The question asks about Website 1.", "1": "This divides the total of both days by Website 1’s clicks only. Website 2’s clicks cost money too.", "2": "This divides the total cost by all the clicks. The two websites charge different amounts, so set up a system."}, "right": "Subtract the equations to cancel the matching term.", "desmos": "Solve both for y: type <b>y = (94.15 − 15x)/29</b> and <b>y = (121.15 − 25x)/29</b>. Click where they cross: (2.7, 1.85).", "numworks": "<b>Grapher</b>: add both lines in y = form (y = (94.15 − 15x)/29 and y = (121.15 − 25x)/29), open the Graph tab, press the <b>Toolbox</b> key and choose <b>Intersection</b>: (2.7, 1.85)."},
41:{"how": ["Quadratic formula with <var>a</var> = 1, <var>b</var> = 32, <var>c</var> = 248.", "<var>x</var> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>−32 ± √(32<sup>2</sup> − 4(1)(248))</span><span>2(1)</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>−32 ± √(1,024 − 992)</span><span>2</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>−32 ± √32</span><span>2</span></span>.", "√32 = √16 · √2 = 4√2. So <var>x</var> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>−32 ± 4√2</span><span>2</span></span> = <b>−16 ± 2√2</b>. That is A."], "why": {"1": "The formula starts with −b, so −32. Starting with +32 flips the signs.", "2": "You forgot to divide by 2a = 2. Divide both −32 and 4√2 by 2.", "3": "Two slips: the formula starts with −b (−32), and everything is divided by 2a = 2."}, "right": "−b, then ± the square root, all over 2a.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = x^2 + 32x + 248</b>. Its x-intercepts are about (−18.83, 0) and (−13.17, 0). −16 − 2√2 ≈ −18.83 and −16 + 2√2 ≈ −13.17.", "numworks": "<b>Equations</b>: type the equation (with =), then <b>Solve the equation</b>: x² + 32x + 248 = 0 gives x = −16 − 2√2 and x = −16 + 2√2."},
42:{"how": ["Decreasing by 30% means keep 70%: <var>b</var> = 0.7. The start is $650: <var>y</var> = 650(0.7)<sup><var>x</var></sup>.", "At <var>x</var> = 0 (when it is bought) the value is 650, so the <b>y-intercept is 650</b>. That is A.", "The graph flattens toward the horizontal asymptote <var>y</var> = 0."], "why": {"1": "455 is the value after 1 year (650 × 0.7). The y-intercept is the value at x = 0: 650.", "2": "An exponential graph has a horizontal asymptote, y = 0, not a vertical one.", "3": "30 is the percent it decreases each year. The value gets close to 0, so the asymptote is y = 0."}, "right": "y-intercept = start. Asymptote y = 0.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = 650(0.7)^x</b> and zoom out. It crosses the y-axis at (0, 650) and flattens toward y = 0."},
43:{"how": ["The boundary line is <var>y</var> = −<var>x</var> + 2: start at (0, 2), then right 1, down 1 to (1, 1).", "≥ has “or equal,” so the line is <b>solid</b>.", "<b>Solution Set:</b> test (0, 0): 0 ≥ −0 + 2 is 0 ≥ 2, which is false. So select the side <b>without</b> (0, 0), above the line."], "miss": "Check three things: the line goes through (0, 2) and (1, 1), it is solid (≥ has “or equal”), and the shading is on the side without (0, 0). You earn 1 point for the line and 1 point for the region.", "right": "y-intercept, slope, solid or dashed, then test (0, 0).", "desmos": "The test version won’t graph inequalities. Type the boundary line <b>y = −x + 2</b> to check your line, then test (0, 0) by hand: 0 ≥ 2 is false, so shade away from (0, 0)."},
44:{"how": ["Find the slope of line <var>m</var> from (0, −3) and (2, 0): run 2, rise 3, slope = <b><span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>2</span></span></b>.", "Parallel lines have the <b>same slope</b>.", "Point <var>P</var> is (−2, 2): 2 = <span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>2</span></span>(−2) + <var>b</var>, so 2 = −3 + <var>b</var> and <var>b</var> = <b>5</b>.", "<var>y</var> = <span class=\"frac\"><span>3</span><span>2</span></span><var>x</var> + 5. That is C."], "why": {"0": "The slope is right, but the x and y of P got switched when finding b. Use x = −2 and y = 2: b = 5.", "1": "−2/3 is the slope of a <b>perpendicular</b> line. Parallel lines have the same slope, 3/2.", "3": "−2/3 is the perpendicular slope, and the x and y of P were switched. Parallel means the same slope, 3/2."}, "right": "Parallel = same slope. Then plug in the point to find b.", "desmos": "Type <b>y = (3/2)x − 3</b> and <b>y = (3/2)x + 5</b>, and the point (−2, 2). The lines never cross, and the second goes through P."},
45:{"how": ["<b>Slide</b> the <var>P</var><var>h</var> to the other side: <var>S</var> − <var>P</var><var>h</var> = 2<var>B</var>.", "<b>Slice</b> both sides by 2: <var>B</var> = <span class=\"frac\"><span><var>S</var> − <var>P</var><var>h</var></span><span>2</span></span>. That is A."], "why": {"1": "Ph is added to 2B, so subtract it. Dividing by Ph and multiplying by 2 undoes the wrong steps.", "2": "Ph is added to 2B, so subtract it, don’t multiply.", "3": "Undo in the right order: subtract Ph (it is added), then divide by 2 (it multiplies B)."}, "right": "Slide what is added, then slice what multiplies.", "check": "Try P = 1, h = 2, B = 3: S = 2 + 6 = 8. Then (8 − 2) ÷ 2 = 3. ✓"},
46:{"how": ["<var>x</var> &gt; 5: the graph starts at <var>x</var> = 5 with an <b>open</b> circle (5 is not included) and goes to the <b>right</b>.", "<var>y</var> &lt; 2: the <var>y</var>-values start at 2 (open) and go <b>down</b>.", "So the graph starts with an open circle at (5, 2) and goes down and to the right. That is B."], "why": {"0": "This goes to the left (x less than 5) and up (y greater than 2).", "2": "This goes down, but to the left, so x is less than 5.", "3": "This goes to the right, but up, so y is greater than 2."}, "right": "Greater than: right (x) or up (y). Less than: left or down."},
47:{"how": ["Put 20 in for <var>x</var>: <var>g</var>(20) = 6(2 · 20 + 7).", "Inside the parentheses first: 2 · 20 = 40, and 40 + 7 = 47.", "Multiply: 6 × 47 = <b>282</b>. That is B."], "why": {"0": "The 6 multiplies the parentheses. It doesn’t add to 2x.", "2": "You added 20 + 7 before multiplying by 2. Multiply first: 2 · 20 = 40, then add 7.", "3": "The 6 multiplies both terms inside: 6 × 2x and 6 × 7."}, "right": "Inside the parentheses first, then multiply.", "desmos": "Type <b>g(x) = 6(2x + 7)</b>, then <b>g(20)</b> on a new line. It shows 282.", "numworks": "<b>Calculation</b>: type 6(2×20 + 7). It shows 282."},
48:{"how": ["The <b>x-intercept</b> is where the line crosses the x-axis: <b>(−2, 0)</b>.", "The <b>y-intercept</b> is where the line crosses the y-axis: <b>(0, 8)</b>.", "That is C."], "why": {"0": "The two are switched. The line crosses the x-axis at −2 and the y-axis at 8.", "1": "The x-intercept is right, but 4 is the slope (up 4 for every 1 to the right). The line crosses the y-axis at 8.", "3": "The y-intercept is right, but 4 is the slope. The line crosses the x-axis at −2."}, "right": "x-intercept on the x-axis, y-intercept on the y-axis."},
49:{"how": ["The <b>zeros</b> are where the graph crosses the <b>x-axis</b>.", "It crosses at <b><var>x</var> = −1</b> and <b><var>x</var> = 3</b>.", "Select those two."], "why": {"0": "1 is the x-value of the vertex, the lowest point. The zeros are on the x-axis.", "1": "−4 is the y-value of the vertex. The zeros are on the x-axis.", "4": "x = 0 is where the y-axis is. The graph crosses the y-axis there, not the x-axis.", "5": "−3 is the y-intercept (where it crosses the y-axis). The zeros are on the x-axis."}, "right": "Zeros: where it crosses the x-axis."},
50:{"how": ["Pick two rows: (3, 21) and (5, 35).", "<b>Run</b> (steps): 5 − 3 = 2. <b>Rise</b> (inches): 35 − 21 = 14.", "Rate = 14 ÷ 2 = <b>7 inches per step</b>. That is C."], "why": {"0": "14 inches is the change over 2 steps. Divide by the change in steps: 14 ÷ 2 = 7.", "1": "This is upside down. Height per step means inches on top.", "3": "This is upside down, and it skips dividing by 2. Inches on top: 14 ÷ 2 = 7."}, "right": "Change in height ÷ change in steps.", "numworks": "<b>Regression</b>: put x in X1 and y in Y1, open the Graph tab, press OK and pick <b>Linear ax+b</b>. The Stats tab shows the equation: a = 7."}
};
